le quotes inside JSON. Our JSON contains single quotes only in backticks? Actually backticks are not single quotes. There\'s no apostrophe. There\'s "cannot". That\'s fine. There\'s "cannot". There\'s no single quote. So we can wrap JSON in single quotes safely.\n\nBut there is a backtick character ` which is fine inside single quotes. So we can do:\n\necho \'{"instance_id":"ai_4411",...}\' > /tmp/result_ai_4411.json && echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT && cat /tmp/result_ai_4411.json\n\nMake sure JSON string does not contain single quote. It contains backticks and double quotes. That\'s okay.\n\nLet\'s construct JSON compactly.\n\nWe\'ll produce command now.'}, 'extra': {'actions': [{'command': 'echo \'{"instance_id":"ai_4411","failure_category":"unresolved","exit_status":"unsolved","api_calls":0,"failure_manifestation":"The test fails to apply the selfheal.patch because the `patch` command is not found in the execution environment.","failure_reason":"During the test, the harness runs `run_test_entrypoint.sh` which attempts to apply a generated patch using the system `patch` utility. The Docker image used for the repository does not have the `patch` binary installed, so the shell reports `patch: command not found`. This error aborts the patch‑application step, causing the test to report a failure before any model interaction occurs. Consequently, no API calls are recorded and the issue remains unresolved.","agent_design_issue":"The test harness assumes the presence of the external `patch` command without declaring it as a required dependency. This design decision resides in `run_test_entrypoint.sh` where the `patch` command is invoked. Because the Docker environment omits the `patch` package, the test cannot proceed. A robust design would either (a) install `patch` in the Dockerfile (e.g., `RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y patch`) or (b) replace the shell call with a pure‑Python implementation (e.g., using the `patch` PyPI package) and update the harness to use that, ensuring the dependency is self‑contained.","affected_component":"environment","defect_class":"environment","severity":"high","confidence":"high"}\' > /tmp/result_ai_4411.json && echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT && cat /tmp/result_ai_4411.json'}], 'response': {'id': 'chatcmpl-afecfa4832f966fc', 'created': 1795043991, 'model': 'gpt-oss-120b', 'object': 'chat.completion', 'choices': [{'finish_reason': 'stop', 'index': 0, 'message': {'content': '<mswea_bash_command>echo \'{"instance_id":"ai_4411","failure_category":"unresolved","exit_status":"unsolved","api_calls":0,"failure_manifestation":"The test fails to apply the selfheal.patch because the `patch` command is not found in the execution environment.","failure_reason":"During the test, the harness runs `run_test_entrypoint.sh` which attempts to apply a generated patch using the system `patch` utility. The Docker image used for the repository does not have the `patch` binary installed, so the shell reports `patch: command not found`. This error aborts the patch‑application step, causing the test to report a failure before any model interaction occurs. Consequently, no API calls are recorded and the issue remains unresolved.","agent_design_issue":"The test harness assumes the presence of the external `patch` command without declaring it as a required dependency. This design decision resides in `run_test_entrypoint.sh` where the `patch` command is invoked. Because the Docker environment omits the `patch` package, the test cannot proceed. A robust design would either (a) install `patch` in the Dockerfile (e.g., `RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y patch`) or (b) replace the shell call with a pure‑Python implementation (e.g., using the `patch` PyPI package) and update the harness to use that, ensuring the dependency is self‑contained.","affected_component":"environment","defect_class":"environment","severity":"high","confidence":"high"}\' > /tmp/result_ai_4411.json && echo COMPLETE_TASK_